findings.body:
Password reset tokens are generated with `Math.random()`, a non-cryptographic PRNG, and then truncated to `Math.floor(... * 1e12).toString(36)` (at most 10^12 values, ~9 characters). An attacker who observes a few tokens can recover the generator state and mint valid reset tokens for any account; the space is also small enough to brute-force. Node `crypto.randomBytes` is unavailable on Edge, but Web Crypto `crypto.getRandomValues` is, and it keeps 256 bits of CSPRNG entropy.

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```
